AGENT QUICK START - MINIMUM SAFE SEQUENCE

Goal: inspect, program, trace and control PicoNIX via MCP.
Do not assume CPython, GNU as, ISO C, Linux shell or POSIX semantics.
Each execute_command call must be finite, noninteractive, and short.

INITIAL INSPECTION (no state changes)
  get_manual()                discover manual topics
  ps                          inspect six task slots
  ls /man                     list available reference pages
  ls /tmp                     inspect existing RAM files
  cat /proc/memory            inspect kernel memory status
  pins                        inspect board/pin assignment
  help cc                     check CLI before writing code

BEFORE CODING
  get_manual(topic='languages/c')      C syntax + limits
  get_manual(topic='c-examples')       correct C examples
  get_manual(topic='c-debug')          debugging checklist
  get_manual(topic='languages/python') Python subset
  get_manual(topic='asm-examples')     Thumb-1 examples
Do NOT generate gcc commands, #include, import or arbitrary libc.

BEFORE HARDWARE CONTROL
  pins
  pwmctl status
  get_manual(topic='gpio-workflow')
  get_manual(topic='pwm-workflow')
  get_manual(topic='i2c-workflow')
Read-only queries first; ask for user approval before changing pins,
PWM frequency, connected actuators, network or persistent settings.

BEFORE TRACING
  ps
  trace on -s
  trace show
  trace on
Use 'trace follow' only on an interactive terminal, never over MCP.

OUTPUT AND TIME LIMITS
  MCP command execution ~3 seconds, output <=4096 bytes.
  Use hexdump FILE OFFSET COUNT (e.g. 64 or 128 bytes).
  A long-running command or continuous /dev reader may stall.
  Repeated HTTP incomplete chunked read is a transport problem;
  retry a smaller bounded command rather than assume command failed.

MCP tools are different from Linux shell commands. get_manual() is
an MCP tool; 'cat /man/README' is a PicoNIX shell command.
The --shell switch is required when starting mcpd for shell tools.
